      "text": "To find the area of region \\( S \\), we start by analyzing the given conditions and the properties of the complex plane.\n\n1. **Hexagon in the Complex Plane:**\n   The hexagon is centered at the origin and has a side length of 1 unit. Since it is regular and has opposite pairs of sides one unit apart, each side length is 1 unit. The distance between any two adjacent vertices (which are at 60 degrees from each other) is 1, and the distance from the center (0,0) to each side is \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\) (since the radius of the circumscribed circle is 1, and the vertices are at \\( \\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\) on the plane).\n\n2. **Equating the Circles:**\n   The vertices of the hexagon divide the unit circle \\(|z| = 1\\) into six arcs, each of length \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\). The region outside the hexagon, denoted by \\(R\\), is the complement of the hexagon in the unit disk. The area of the unit disk is \\( \\pi \\), and the area of the hexagon can be calculated using the formula for the area of a regular hexagon with side length \\( s \\):\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{hexagon}} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot s^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot 1^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n   \\]\n   The area of region \\(R\\) is then:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } R = \\pi - \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Image Region \\( S \\):**\n   The image region \\( S \\) is the set of points \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\) for all \\( z \\in R \\). The transformation \\( w = \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps the region outside the unit circle to outside the unit circle, but scales it by a factor. The key observation is that the transformation \\( w = \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps the unit disk to the interior of the unit circle, and the region outside the unit circle to the exterior of the unit circle. Since the hexagon is mapped to the region outside the unit disk, under the transformation \\( w = \\frac{1}{z} \\), the region \\( R \\) (which is outside the hexagon) is mapped to the interior of the unit disk.\n\n4. **Area of the Image Region \\( S \\):**\n   The area of the unit disk is \\( \\pi \\), and the region outside the hexagon \\( R \\) has half the area of the unit disk. When we apply the transformation \\( w = \\frac{1}{z} \\), the area is scaled by \\( |1|^{-2} = 1 \\), so the area of the transformed region \\( S \\) is the same as the area of the unit disk:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } S = \\pi.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of \\( S \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\pi}\\).",
