      "text": "We start with a regular hexagon centered at the origin with sides of length $1$.  Consider a radius given by $\\large z \\longmapsto re^{i\\theta}$ for $ r\\geq 1$,\nwhere $0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$ is an angular coordinate for $P :=1+e^{i(2\\pi/3)}$.  The set of argnded $P$'s forms the boundary circle (r=$r_\\circ=2(N=16)$), \nin which each point is assumed to lie on $|z|=r_\\circ$.  The image of $P$ is a circle centered at $p=1/e^{i(2\\pi/3)}$, with radius\n$\\large |z / P| = \\sqrt{1/r_\\circ}=1/\\sqrt{2(R=2(1/2)}).\\ [r=0$ is uninteresting].  In terms of $t$, we have $|t P |=2$ for $0<t\\leq 2$; \nthus, if $Ron$ is the upper right megure of the unit circle ($(\\cos \\pi/6,\\sin \\pi/6)$), then $R ~\\supset~ S~=~\\bigcup _{0 \\leq t \\leq 2} \\{r,(\\cos 2at\\pi/3+\\sin 2at\\pi/3)t e^{i\\farsidemptyat\\pi /3}\\}$ \n!= $R(2())).$  The area of $S$ is given by\n\\begin{align*}\n[2r_0-1]&\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{3} +2\\sin\\frac{9\\pi}{6}br_N \\\\\n{\\testfor{change}}&\\quad+ \\frac{1}{2}\\int _1^{2 }\\sin 2at\\pi/3 \\ dt\\\\\n=2\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{3}-\\big[\\frac{1}{2}\\cos \\frac{5\\pi}{6}- 2 \\cos \\frac{13\\pi}{6}\\big]\\\\\n=2-\\big[\\frac{4}{2}+ 2 .\\frac{1}{3} +\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{2}{5}\\big]\\\\\n=\\frac{27\\pi-79}{24},\n\\end{align*}\nnote that $\\big[\\frac{1}{2}\\cos \\frac{5\\pi}{6}- 2 \\cos \\frac{13\\pi}{6}\\big]=0$, therefore it is accurate to omit the integration term.  Note also that $\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\sin2at\\pi/3 \\ dt=1$ since it represents the area of an isosceles triangle with base length $2$ and height $2$.",
